WEBVTT

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:22.360
Story and Planning by HEADGEAR

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.290
First Platoon, drop complete.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.860
LZ secured, over.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:32.070
Airlift Wing, this is FAC. Begin drop of second wave.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:46.970
Original Concept by Yuuki Masami

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.050
FAC, this is Zero One. Target in sight.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.060
Target bearing zero two, distance 1200.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.060
Target verified.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.830
Character Design by Akemi Takada

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.680
Assistant Director: Koji Sawai

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.390
Target moving to your zero-three, 40 km/h.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.040
Commence your attack run once you have visual contact.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.330
Ground forces, be advised.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.650
FIRE!

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.520
Producers:      Shin Unozawa
Taro Maki
Makoto Kubo

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:57.290
Directed by Mamoru Oshii

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.560
That's Kisarazu Artificial Island Number 1 over there.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.970
When it's done, the reclaimed area will be 450 thousand square meters.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.570
Along with Island Number 2, under construction off Kawasaki,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.800
it forms the core installation of this project.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.880
Once complete, they'll move on to the final phase:

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.110
the construction of a huge dike over 15km long...

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:43.480
...connecting Kisarazu to Kawasaki via the artificial islands.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:49.530
When done, they'll be able to open a huge beltway around Tokyo,

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:53.650
and use tides to drain the bay through a dozen or so sluices.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.700
Between the lowering sea levels and land reclamation,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.900
we'll secure more than 45,000 hectares inside Tokyo Bay over the next ten years.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.260
Land shortages won't be an issue for Tokyo in the 21st century.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:07.600
Babylon, the eternal city...

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:11.050
To reinvent itself for this century, Tokyo has undertaken...

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:14.910
...history's largest maritime construction: the Babylon Project.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:19.750
With all the red tape involved, they weren't sure if they'd be done by century's end,

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.550
but the advent of Labor technology changed all that.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.390
They say basic engineering issues were ironed out in the 1980s,

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:34.840
but the tech wouldn't have matured so quickly if this project hadn't needed so many Labors.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:41.540
In fact, they're using over 3600 Labors in this project's construction zones.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:46.010
That's 45% of all the Labors in Japan.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:51.320
Just one Labor is as productive as dozens of highly skilled workers.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.990
This project makes extensive use of them.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:59.390
Yesterday's science fiction is now a reality.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:07.400
Mind you, now there's criminal activities involving Labors. Never saw that coming...

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:10.030
But then, that's your specialty, isn't it?

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.750
Okay, there is it now. Look at 5 o'clock low.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.040
It's 500 meters long, and reaches 150 meters above sea level.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:28.020
It's the service platform for all the Labors in this project area.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.860
We call it the Ark.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.430
So, why do they call it the Ark?

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:36.520
How should I know?

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.270
Shutting down systems 01 through 05.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.800
Display functioning normally...

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:49.400
The system is working perfectly, right?

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.540
It certainly seems to be for now.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.840
Oh, please, what's gonna go wrong?

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:58.410
We ran dozens of checks before we even brought it over.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:00.250
What was your guy's name... Mr. Shiba, right?

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.990
Being a stickler is fine, but he's overdoing it.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:06.650
The ones he took overseas haven't had any major issues, right?

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:12.470
The Hachioji factory's nagging us to get the go-ahead to roll out the rest.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.330
I want to try booting it from the backup this time.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:16.670
Wait a sec.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:20.410
Two members of the 2nd Unit are here for you.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.180
From the 2nd Unit?

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:28.840
...damn!

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.890
How about that, Noa?! Wasn't this worth the trip?

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.650
I dunno... He's not my type.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:37.660
What?!

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:42.320
I got us out there 'cause you wanted to see this thing, and that's your attitude?!

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:43.920
I never said that!

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:45.930
Let's wrap it up for today.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.060
Roger that!

00:08:57.050 --> 00:09:00.640
Officers Shinohara and Izumi reporting to escort Cmdr. Nagumo back to the station!

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:04.690
You came sooner than I expected. I wasn't informed that you'd be my escort.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:09.150
We got a lift from a civilian chopper that came by the SV2 station house.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:12.760
The maintenance crew's prized speedboat is on the way.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:16.790
So, anyway... Until they arrive, would you mind if we...

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:21.190
You sniffed this out already? I suppose it's no wonder, you being Gotoh's people...

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:24.740
Perhaps you'd like to test it out?

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.210
Although Officer Izumi doesn't seem very impressed.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:28.910
Uh, I think she overheard us...

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:33.070
Why'd you say something stupid like that? She's gonna remember this...

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:37.620
I only said what I thought. Don't you think he looks kinda evil?

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:39.190
That's all subjective.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.790
I hear it'll be designated the Type Zero once in service.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:44.620
The final variant of the 98 series.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:50.190
More precisely, it's the first designed from the ground up to run on HOS.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:51.900
Hoss? What's that?

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:55.400
Jeez, you have no interest in what makes Labors tick, do you?

00:09:55.710 --> 00:10:00.010
You know about the core system program that runs Labors, right?

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:01.810
Oh, you're talking about the OS.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:03.900
HOS is the revolutionary operating system...

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:06.540
...that Shinohara Heavy Industries released two months ago.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:10.820
It makes even existing Labors 30% more productive just by upgrading the OS.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.390
It's a real quantum leap.

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:16.590
Hyper Operating System. H.O.S. "HOS"!

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.920
Most Labors have been upgraded to it by now.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:22.330
Sure, but in the end, it's all up to the operator's...

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:23.420
- ...brains and guts!
- ...brains and guts!

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:24.520
Damn straight.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.770
Paging Commander Nagumo of the SV2.

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:30.970
Your boat has arrived. Please go to Deck A-15.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.310
Oh, man, they're here already?

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.070
Come on, let's go.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:46.990
Level B, Block 5 maintenance crew, please report to sitting room 7.

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.750
Whoa! I feel like Gulliver in the land of giants!

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:53.320
Well, the whole place is designed around Labors.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:57.060
Hurry up or I'll leave you behind!

00:10:57.170 --> 00:10:58.290
- Coming!
- Coming!

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:04.770
Level C transport support group, be advised. Shipment transferring from E28 to F151-

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:08.470
Commander Nagumo, if I can ask you a question...

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.380
I don't know much about this place, if that's what you're wondering.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.890
No, ma'am. Um, about the Type Zero... What's it doing here?

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:20.780
I take it you're aware of the NYPD's experimental Labor Unit,

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:24.190
and the three Type Zeros sent there?

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.490
Something about how Kanuka might be made the unit commander, right?

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:28.220
I'm not sure I like the sound of that, but go on...

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.640
Mr. Shige, who's over there acting as a technical advisor, contacted us.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:37.440
He asked that the Type Zero be given a thorough examination. By the SV2.

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:41.510
SHI has a factory line here, and it's near the SV2 station house.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:43.800
Plus, it's much easier to keep a lid on things here.

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.210
Have you found any problems?

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:49.220
Of course not. If we had, there would have been a big commotion.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:53.850
But the request came from Mr. Shige, so we weren't about to dismiss it.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.860
The retraining session has been extended?!

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:13.040
Sir, you said yourself that my men would be back on duty within the week.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:16.870
Yes. But the situation has changed.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:22.080
The 1st Unit's Type Zero retraining session will be extended by one week.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:24.690
Sir, I must demand an adequate explanation.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:28.190
We all know that the 2nd Unit is hardly ideal to begin with,

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:31.660
but to overwork them even more is begging for disaster!

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:34.760
If you would be so kind as to call back my officers,

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:37.660
I have absolute confidence in the 1st Unit's performance!

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:40.860
Nagumo, the higher-ups have made their decision.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:43.030
I have no say in the matter.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:49.380
Then I will petition the Security Bureau Director himself to have my unit returned.

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:50.310
Excuse me.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:51.710
Hold on.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:57.680
I am speaking on my own discretion. This is not to leave this room, understand?

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:02.890
Two days ago, an experimental military Labor belonging to the JGSDF 7th Corps...

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.420
...suddenly went berserk while undergoing wind tunnel testing.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:09.130
It broke through the perimeter and escaped off-base.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:14.000
They had to deploy their prized airborne Labor units to stop it.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:15.930
The cause of the incident is unknown,

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:18.600
and with the Labor turned to scrap, their chances of finding out are slim.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.140
It seems they were in a bit of a panic.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:22.710
But what does that have to do with our situation?

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:25.240
The rampaging Labor was manufactured by Shinohara Heavy Industries.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:30.250
And like the Type Zero, it was designed to run on the new system.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:35.550
They're keeping this part quiet, but the Labor was supposedly unmanned at the time.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:38.390
Is that explanation adequate enough for you?

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:42.530
This could become a political quagmire.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.830
Everybody wants to take a prudent approach, you might say.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:48.060
You've got to be kidding!

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.330
Are you serious?

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:54.740
- This is ridiculous... Cut us some slack.
- Sorry, Tamiko, honey...

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.470
Now what the hell kind of attitude is that?!

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:03.420
We've got to protect the city from evildoers while our 1st Unit seniors are away!

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:06.720
Yeah, but who's gonna protect OUR human rights?

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:07.980
Cops don't have any!

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:09.860
This must be really tough on you, Mr. Shinshi...

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.720
I've only been able to go home three times this month.

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.460
This is starting to threaten my marriage.

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.730
That's what you get for getting hitched, you idiot.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.670
Stationed away from your family on a strip of reclaimed land...

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:24.140
Don't we seem to get dispatched a lot more these days?

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.970
7th Zone reports a 205 in progress in Shitaya, Taitoh Ward!

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:31.070
2nd Unit, deploy immediately.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:34.180
I repeat, 7th Zone reports a 205 in progress in Shitaya, Taitoh Ward!

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:35.050
Figures, perfect timing...

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:36.550
2nd Unit, deploy immediately!

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:36.550
Let's go!

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:39.680
Get the trailers out! Prep the revolver cannons!

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:43.150
Anybody who's fooling around is gonna wish they'd never been born!

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:50.700
A Labor operator has lost control of his machine,

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:53.070
and the rampaging Labor is rapidly approaching this area!

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:56.000
All citizens, please evacuate immediately!

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.100
Move! I said move, damn it!

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:02.300
You wanna die or something?! Get the hell out of here right now!

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:04.210
Crap! Here it comes!

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:14.220
Calling SV2 2nd Unit, now en route. This is Sakura-fubuki 12 overhead.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:19.730
Target ID'd as a Yotsubishi Heavy Industries Tyrant 2000 construction Labor.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:22.460
It's moving east from its construction site on Kokusai-dori Street at 4 km/h,

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:24.730
demolishing neighborhoods as it goes!

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:30.270
Shinohara, head along the river and set up ahead of the target.

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:31.030
Yes, sir!

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:33.840
Start up Labor 2. Begin pursuit immediately.

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:36.540
Roger that. Booting up!

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:39.540
Mr. Oota! Don't forget, saving lives is your highest priority!

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:41.380
You understand what that means, right?!

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:44.550
Shove it! Don't second guess on-the-scene decisions!

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:48.620
Why don't you just tell him flat-out not to use his gun?

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.120
Raise him up...

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:14.520
The Yotsubishi Tyrant 2000 is a real heavyweight among construction Labors.

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:17.420
It's slow, but it packs a lot of horsepower. Keep your guard up, all right?!

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:20.660
I really hate this part of the job...

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.420
Hey!

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:28.030
Over there, over there! That bastard crushed my squad car!

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:29.330
Go give him some payback!

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:31.420
Leave it to me!

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:36.940
You moron! I hope he flattens you, too!

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.700
Somebody, help! Someone stop this thing!

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:47.150
Attention, operator of the berserk Labor! Do you read me?!

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:51.310
This is the SV2, 2nd Unit! We're here to get you out of this mess!

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:54.920
2nd Unit...? Oh, dear God!

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:56.650
What the hell?!

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:00.820
I treat you decently as a taxpayer, and that's how you react?!

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:02.760
You stinking bastard!

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.840
Cmdr. Gotoh, this is Sakura-fubuki 12 monitoring the situation.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:16.000
Your 102 is roughing up the rampaging Labor in a packed residential area!

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:18.780
Doesn't seem to be having much effect, though...

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:20.510
Whoa, he just kicked it!

00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:23.680
Followed by a drop kick! Could you please rein this guy in, sir?

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:29.760
Sensors picking them up! Here they come, Noa!

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:33.820
Hey! Do something about the operator!

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:36.690
This damn thing refuses to stop!

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.570
Oota! Use your stun baton!

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:48.680
Aim for the RAM unit in its upper left torso! Take it out in one strike!

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:50.870
Eat this!

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:55.180
Okay, that's that! Let's head home and grab some lunch!

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:58.410
What the hell?!

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.890
Wha-? What the hell is going on here?!

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:17.570
Mr. Oota! Don't!

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.200
All trailers, prepare for pickup.

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:23.170
Prepare to meet your maker!!

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:31.650
Operation complete.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:34.880
Target completely destroyed. Subsequent collateral damage is extensive.

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:36.080
I say again...

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:38.990
Easy! Easy!

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:43.660
Wash it thoroughly and blow dry the whole thing!

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:45.830
Make sure you double-check the electrical systems!

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:46.660
Yessir!

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:49.360
Where's the moron who dropped the Labor into the river?!

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.000
He's out back, taking a bath.

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:52.740
Bring him to me when he's done.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:53.840
Yessir!

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:55.270
Rebooted?

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:59.910
A Labor somehow restarted itself all on its own?

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:02.340
That's rather hard to believe.

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:06.060
I saw it myself, and I don't really believe it, either.

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:09.720
And that moron's shooting spree didn't leave us much to investigate.

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:11.520
Isn't it possible that you just imagined it?

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:14.420
You've had one dispatch after another lately. No one could blame you.

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:18.020
Did the operator say what caused the incident?

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:22.730
Same story as always. It started moving on its own while he was working.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:26.330
Of course he'd say that, after the mess he's made.

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:28.310
But don't you think it's a bit strange?!

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:30.470
Before two months ago, this hardly ever happened!

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:33.010
We've had 22 cases so far this month alone!

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:35.980
Having to deal with all this makes US wanna go berserk.

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:40.310
How should I write this up in my report?

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:45.260
You know damn well the brass won't stand for that.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:48.620
Besides, the manufacturers and the top brass issued a joint statement.

00:19:48.730 --> 00:19:51.500
They say flat-out that accidents caused by programs running amok...

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:54.130
...simply aren't possible with current Labor technology.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:58.030
So, "Operator error". Case closed.

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:01.840
Testimony from the operator and Labor officers with dubious reputations...

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:04.140
...won't be enough to get the police to change their ways.

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:09.020
Noa!

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:10.550
What?

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:13.260
This is my last one, so no mooching, got it?!

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:17.990
Listen, get me all the dispatch records and incident reports from this month.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.060
Huh? What do you want all those for?

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:21.290
Just do it, okay?

00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:25.460
Cap! Can I use the Fridge?!

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:26.970
The computer room?

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:31.970
- Permission granted.
- Thank you!

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:36.240
You certainly excel at getting people to do your bidding without their knowledge.

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:39.510
You have a vested interest in this, too. Your Type Zeroes are involved.

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:43.280
Commander! Detective Matsui from the Criminal Investigation Bureau is on the phone!

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:45.790
Good deal. Patch it through to my office!

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:47.190
What are you up to this time?

00:20:47.290 --> 00:20:49.090
Wouldn't you like to know?

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:20.780
Yes. Shigeo Shiba from the Tokyo City Police.

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:24.720
Special Vehicles Section 2.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:25.720
Absent?

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:28.850
Oh, yeah... Kanuka Clancy? Yes.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:40.030
You're still at it?

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:42.870
That you, Noa? You'll catch cold if you stand there for long.

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:47.100
Try to get some sleep. There's no telling when we'll get called out next.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:48.640
'Kay...

00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:50.870
- You too, Asuma.
- Sure.

00:23:09.430 --> 00:23:10.400
Amazing!

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:14.430
Most of them aren't very big. I couldn't give them enough attention.

00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:18.410
You sure have a green thumb, Hiromi-chan. Everyone will be thrilled!

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:20.310
Ms. Izumi, please go ahead.

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:23.440
These aren't going to market, so only pick the ripe ones.

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:24.310
Okay.

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:26.810
By the way, where's Asuma?

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:29.250
Didn't he say that he wanted to help out?

00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:31.580
He said something about going to the Commanders' office

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:34.160
He was up all night doing research,

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:35.950
so I guess it might have something to do with that.

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:44.060
Hey, can I share some of these with the Commanders?

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:46.630
I went through all the cases of berserk Labors,

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:49.830
cross-referenced their manufacturers, their maintenance records,

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:52.570
the operators' backgrounds, and everything else available.

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:55.570
But I found only one common denominator:

00:23:55.780 --> 00:24:01.310
all of the runaway Labors, regardless of manufacturer, were running Shinohara HOS.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:05.990
But over 80% of all Labors have upgraded to HOS.

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:10.190
Yes, but from a statistical standpoint, 100% is a number that can't be ignored.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:14.260
And another thing: the increase in incidents began two months ago.

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:17.530
That coincides perfectly with HOS' release date.

00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:20.100
The evidence points to one clear conclusion.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.900
There must be a fatal flaw with HOS.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:25.470
We should report this through the proper channels...

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:28.540
...and force SHI to release the source code!

00:24:30.410 --> 00:24:31.470
Commander!

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:36.710
SHI released HOS with the aim of cornering the market.

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:42.080
If the OS turned out to be flawed, it could cripple the company.

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:47.460
Besides, the pencil pushers that approve OS aren't dimwits.

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:52.160
If there was a bug in HOS, they'd have found it while debugging.

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:53.030
But, sir!

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:54.530
Just listen.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:56.540
Let's say you're right.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:00.340
Let's say there's a link between HOS and this rash of malfunctioning Labors.

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:04.580
And what if there aren't any bugs in HOS? What then?

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:05.900
Sir?

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:11.850
What if the Labors aren't running amok because of an error,

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:14.860
but because something was deliberately programmed into the system?

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:16.430
Understand?

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:28.940
Would you mind reading this? It's Mr. Matsui's preliminary report.

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.570
"Investigation file for Ei'ichi Hoba"...

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:39.770
Hoba? You mean Ei'ichi Hoba, the programmer?

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:41.610
Ei'ichi Hoba.

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:44.650
The ace programmer of SHI's Software Development Department.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:47.980
He's the genius who wrote HOS almost singlehandedly.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:49.690
Or rather, he was...

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:51.550
Presumed age, 30.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:56.190
Returned to Japan from MIT in '97. Hired immediately by SHI.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:59.270
Address, unknown. Background, unknown. Family members, unknown.

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:01.590
Height, approximately 170 cm.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:05.110
Distinctive features and medical history, unknown.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:06.500
What is this?

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:11.880
All his records were missing not only from SHI's Human Resources computers,

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:15.680
but also from his school and all municipal computers.

00:26:16.090 --> 00:26:17.710
If this isn't incriminating, I don't know what is.

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:21.880
Then that settles it. We just have to get our hands on this guy and-

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.520
Unfortunately, we can't do that.

00:26:23.630 --> 00:26:25.390
We can't?! Why not?!

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:29.900
He was sent out to work at SHI's maintenance line at the Ark.

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:33.830
But just five days ago, he jumped into the sea.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:36.170
They never recovered his body.

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:43.780
Boy, we blew it this time.

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:46.970
This happens just as we started suspecting HOS...

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:50.750
If only we'd gotten started on this sooner... Dammit.

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:55.450
Oh well. Maybe it's a bug, maybe it's a plot.

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:58.190
Either way, the case against HOS is pretty strong.

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:03.760
For now, we'll have Mr. Matsui keep following up on Hoba,

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:06.340
who's both the creator of HOS and our prime suspect, and-

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:08.700
Shinohara? Do you have something to add?

00:27:08.810 --> 00:27:12.260
You suspected that HOS figured into this all along, didn't you?

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:16.640
Man, do I look like a putz, working all night!

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:18.280
If it ends here, then yes.

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:22.220
I'd like you to check the data to find out what makes the Labors go haywire.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:23.790
- You up to it? Sir!
- Yes, sir!

00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:28.020
And keep this under wraps. Not a word to anyone else.

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:29.260
Is that clear?

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:31.690
Um... Why's that, sir?

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:34.460
Don't you remember?

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:40.330
Just last month Mr. Shige was ordered to upgrade our Type 98s to HOS.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:46.800
We have to assume our '98s are also at risk until we can prove HOS isn't connected.

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:49.140
But if a dispatch call comes in, we still have to go.

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:52.850
No point having them worry about something they can't control.

00:27:53.280 --> 00:27:55.380
I think I'm gonna take a quick trip to Hachioji.

00:27:55.490 --> 00:27:56.350
Cap...

00:27:56.790 --> 00:28:01.850
Just gotta check something myself. I won't get underfoot.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:03.660
Commander! I'd like to go, too!

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:05.020
Please, can I?

00:28:05.330 --> 00:28:06.920
I'm sure I don't have to say this, but...

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:08.360
I'll watch myself!

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:21.740
Quit dragging your feet! Let's go!

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:23.330
Coming!

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:27.750
Is this wise?

00:28:27.850 --> 00:28:32.290
They'll be back tonight. If a call comes in, I'll take the helm myself.

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:34.450
No, I didn't mean that. Him.

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:37.460
He gets worked up over anything regarding SHI.

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:39.860
You might get him into trouble.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.900
Talk about irony... The father is the CEO of the leading Labor manufacturer,

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:46.460
and his only son is an officer in the Labor force.

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:49.070
And on top of that, they're always fighting.

00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:53.010
In either case, it's Shinohara's call.

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:56.110
I don't like coercing or ordering people to do something.

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:02.650
Lines 3 and 4, rollout complete.

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:05.120
Lines 3 and 4, rollout complete.

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:08.880
Floor crews, prepare main hoists A through F.

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:14.090
With production slated for the Babylon Project mostly complete,

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:16.490
our focus now is on exports.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:20.730
We've just increased this production line's output by 20%.

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:22.430
It's a sight to see, isn't it?

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:23.200
Goodness...

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:27.970
No matter how many times I see this, it still feels like a dream.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:31.310
Old man Shinohara and I started out fifty years ago from a small workshop factory,

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:34.440
providing spare parts for the US occupation forces' trucks.

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:36.920
Then we went from automotive parts to manufacturing equipment.

00:29:37.020 --> 00:29:40.920
The year Asuma was born, we started developing industrial robots.

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:44.020
And now these mechanical behemoths called Labors roll off the lines.

00:29:44.390 --> 00:29:47.120
Then there's me, clueless about what's going on beneath me.

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:49.630
I've tried my best to study and keep up,

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:52.860
but with things like system engineering and fifth generation computers...

00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:55.430
Frankly, I can't make heads or tails of them.

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:58.270
Pretty sad as an engineer, I must say.

00:29:58.370 --> 00:30:01.210
It's the same with me.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:04.940
They might worship me as some kind of vehicle maintenance god,

00:30:05.050 --> 00:30:07.540
but I'm just a novice when it comes to software.

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:11.110
I can't keep up with Shige and the kids under me.

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:13.150
No, please, Mr. Sakaki...

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:17.180
There's no point in two obsolete engineers licking each other's wounds.

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:19.260
I'm just telling it like it is.

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:24.000
But one thing won't change no matter how advanced technology gets.

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:26.430
The people who make the machines. The people who maintain them.

00:30:26.540 --> 00:30:27.660
The people who use them.

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:32.640
As long as people don't mess up, machines won't mess with you.

00:30:33.240 --> 00:30:36.230
Mr. Jitsuyama, I'm not here as the head of the maintenance crew...

00:30:36.350 --> 00:30:39.210
...coming to meet the factory foreman of his supplier.

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:43.650
We're both engineers who've spent more time with machines than with our wives.

00:30:43.750 --> 00:30:46.420
So be frank with me.

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:51.020
Your company's HOS... Is it kosher?

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:54.530
Of all the things to ask...

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:58.090
We've staked our company's reputation on HOS...

00:30:58.300 --> 00:31:00.170
It's a real hit at all the construction sites.

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:05.500
They also say that HOS shortened the Babylon Project's timeline by 30%, and-

00:31:05.610 --> 00:31:07.540
That isn't what I asked.

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:12.010
How about it?

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:15.650
It's absolutely safe! I personally guarantee it!

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:27.920
Okay, copy's finished.

00:31:35.070 --> 00:31:37.060
A copy of the HOS master disk?!

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.500
How about ' Ei'ichi Hoba '...

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:07.860
Ehoba [Jehovah]?

00:32:54.050 --> 00:32:56.850
It just keeps spreading! We can't tell how it's-

00:32:57.320 --> 00:32:58.720
What?! But sir, if we do that, it'll-

00:32:58.850 --> 00:33:02.650
Just do it! Cut the power to the central server!

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:05.820
Our production lines here are connected to our other factories!

00:33:06.460 --> 00:33:07.950
That dumbass...

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:16.670
Oh, man, that scared the hell out of me!

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:19.370
You didn't leave any traces behind, right?

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:20.640
Of course not.

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:24.640
But the only people with the access code are Uncle Jitsuyama and me.

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:26.010
They'll figure it out eventually.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:28.520
Guess we can't show our faces there for a while...

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:30.350
Still, we sure hit the jackpot.

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:33.250
If a copy of the master disk contains a program that can cause havoc like that,

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:36.960
it's obvious that no one at SHI has cracked HOS' encryption yet.

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:42.530
So 8000 or so Labors around Tokyo are running a suspect OS,

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:44.070
and God only knows what's in it.

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:45.530
Just wonderful...

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:49.440
Listen, Asuma, the smarter folks'll start raising a fuss about this.

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:52.600
Even our little lady will catch on eventually.

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:55.140
We gotta clear this up soon, okay?

00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:56.110
Yes, sir!

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:36.700
You can see it from here, too.

00:36:36.810 --> 00:36:38.430
Huh? Oh, that.

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:41.300
All of his apartments have views of skyscrapers.

00:36:41.610 --> 00:36:44.670
But then, what kind of view isn't exactly rare in Tokyo.

00:36:44.780 --> 00:36:46.370
I'd say it's just a coincidence.

00:36:47.550 --> 00:36:50.450
I honestly don't get this Hoba guy.

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:54.120
He programmed Labor software, so he made big bucks, right?

00:36:54.790 --> 00:36:57.260
So why live in ratholes like this?

00:36:57.360 --> 00:36:59.660
And 26 of them in two years...

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:04.460
It's a great way to hide your tracks in a city like this.

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:06.470
The buildings get torn down for redevelopment,

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:09.560
and even of one's left standing, it'll be abandoned.

00:37:09.740 --> 00:37:11.970
Hard to ask questions when nobody's around.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:14.130
Don't you think it's strange, though?

00:37:14.410 --> 00:37:16.940
If he had everything planned out,

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:21.170
why leave JUST his address changes in the municipal computers?

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:25.310
He hacked both his employer's system and his school overseas,

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:27.480
and wiped his personal information.

00:37:27.590 --> 00:37:29.650
Maybe we're supposed to notice something.

00:37:32.130 --> 00:37:35.120
Uh, detectives, do you mind?

00:37:35.230 --> 00:37:37.260
Sure, thanks for your trouble. Sorry to keep you.

00:37:48.610 --> 00:37:49.580
Thanks.

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:54.710
Maybe there's something he wanted to show people.

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:57.110
People who take the bait and start snooping around.

00:37:57.220 --> 00:37:58.520
People like us.

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:02.390
Show us what? And why?

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:06.520
Looks like we'll have to play along if we want to find out.

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:14.130
Mr. Matsui, are you sure we should be doing this?

00:38:14.240 --> 00:38:18.200
The Chief will let it got for a while. That's how it works.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:20.040
That's what I don't get.

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:23.630
Why's the CIB snooping around for the Security Bureau?

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:26.610
What's the story with that man, Gotoh?

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:30.050
He's called Gotoh the Razor. The guy's notorious at headquarters.

00:38:30.150 --> 00:38:33.920
If he's so sharp, why'd he get exiled to the reclaimed area?

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:37.320
Well, you see... He was TOO sharp.

00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:40.390
Come on, let's go.

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:42.560
Oh, wait!

00:38:50.870 --> 00:38:54.240
Here's where they've gone berserk over the past two months.

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:56.810
Pretty concentrated, aren't they?

00:38:57.010 --> 00:39:00.640
The Riverside redevelopment zone.
The Shinjuku New Metropolitan Center.

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:02.510
Ariake Harbor City.

00:39:03.250 --> 00:39:04.450
Hold on, Shinohara.

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:07.650
Those areas have lots of Labors to begin with.

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:09.650
That's what I thought, too.

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:12.290
But here's the distribution of all the Labors in Tokyo.

00:39:13.730 --> 00:39:16.130
I see. This is getting interesting.

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:19.700
Other locations have a lot of Labors, too,

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:23.600
but the problems only happen in these limited areas.

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:27.900
So something in those areas is the trigger.

00:39:28.010 --> 00:39:29.640
Well? Any ideas?

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:32.080
I've filtered the data every way I can think of...

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:34.310
...to try to pinpoint the common denominator.

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:38.150
But it's all to damn vague... I've got nothing.

00:39:38.820 --> 00:39:40.880
I wish we had Mr. Shige here for this...

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:45.250
Shinohara, why don't you take a break?

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:46.990
You didn't sleep much last night either, did you?

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:48.460
I can't do that, sir.

00:39:48.830 --> 00:39:51.730
Not when we still don't know what might set off our own '98s.

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:54.160
Look, just go get some fresh air. Now.

00:39:56.140 --> 00:39:58.110
Yesterday's dream has become today's goal,

00:39:58.310 --> 00:40:03.210
and forges onward to become tomorrow's reality.

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:07.110
Babylon Project moving us towards a prosperous tomorrow!

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:09.650
A challenge for the 21st century!

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:13.890
Here you go, guys!

00:40:16.130 --> 00:40:17.820
One each, okay?

00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:19.020
Vitamins! Vitamins!

00:40:19.130 --> 00:40:20.120
Vitamins!

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:23.920
Hey, Asuma! Why don't you have one?

00:40:24.430 --> 00:40:26.130
Have what? A tomato?

00:40:26.240 --> 00:40:30.040
Don't give me that. You need the vitamins more than anyone.

00:40:31.010 --> 00:40:33.170
I'd prefer something that'll put some fire in my belly...

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:40.640
What's wrong? They're good and clean.

00:40:43.150 --> 00:40:44.920
Noa, did you-

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:48.820
C'mon!

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:50.450
What's with you?!

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:52.820
Just come on! We're going on a date!

00:40:52.930 --> 00:40:54.730
- Hey!
- Don't fight it!

00:40:54.830 --> 00:40:56.490
What's the big idea?!

00:40:56.630 --> 00:40:59.290
A date!? What the hell?!

00:41:00.240 --> 00:41:02.470
- You lucky bastard!
- Drop dead!

00:41:03.070 --> 00:41:06.630
Hey, do we really have permission to leave the station house?

00:41:06.740 --> 00:41:09.210
The Commander himself told me to get some fresh air,

00:41:09.310 --> 00:41:11.780
so that's the same thing, right?

00:41:11.980 --> 00:41:14.740
Maybe so, but still in uniform?

00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:17.920
Plus, we're riding double without helmets!

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:20.180
Aren't we asking for trouble?

00:41:20.290 --> 00:41:25.090
I got it covered! I checked the local patrol schedules!

00:41:25.190 --> 00:41:27.030
That's not what I mean!

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:27.820
What?

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:30.830
I said that's not what I mean, damn it!

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:32.530
Then what did you mean?!

00:41:32.630 --> 00:41:34.230
Oh, forget it! Just floor it!

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:35.500
You got it!

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:45.810
Here we are. One large deluxe Japanesque mix pizza.

00:41:45.910 --> 00:41:47.580
Oh, wow!

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:52.880
Um, are you sure about this? We kinda stand out, you know?

00:41:52.990 --> 00:41:56.150
Just act natural, and no one will notice.

00:41:56.260 --> 00:42:00.320
Yeah, you're right. I'm gonna dig in!

00:42:01.630 --> 00:42:05.330
So how come you're treating me to dinner all of a sudden?

00:42:05.670 --> 00:42:07.360
Uh, no real reason.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:10.640
Say, Noa?

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:11.730
Yeah?

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:15.610
How's Alphonse been lately?

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:21.450
Great, of course. Why do you ask?

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:24.110
No reason, just curious.

00:42:24.220 --> 00:42:26.980
Alphonse is always in top condition.

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:31.150
Mr. Sakaki and the crew take great care of him.

00:42:32.060 --> 00:42:36.390
He does everything I ask... He works just fine.

00:42:36.670 --> 00:42:38.330
He won't go berserk!

00:42:54.680 --> 00:42:56.410
I knew it, you WERE eavesdropping...

00:42:58.850 --> 00:43:03.290
It's okay, right? Alphonse won't go berserk, will he?

00:43:03.730 --> 00:43:04.820
I don't know.

00:43:04.930 --> 00:43:13.030
We don't know what sets them off. The only one who did know is dead.

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:17.130
Maybe if we got rid of HOS...

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:21.010
Only the manufacturer can reinstall the OS.

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:23.300
That requires the proper paperwork.

00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:28.480
The top brass will never OK that unless we can explain the problem.

00:43:28.580 --> 00:43:32.750
But if we don't do something, Alphonse might go berserk!

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:36.020
If he does, he'll be destroyed, right?

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:39.360
Just like all the others that Mr. Oota and I had to put down.

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:45.700
Noa, please, hang in there. I'll find the answer.

00:43:50.110 --> 00:43:52.940
"Forward and Backup are two-in-one."

00:43:53.810 --> 00:43:56.440
"The Pilot shall follow Command's orders"!

00:43:56.810 --> 00:43:59.470
Asuma, you have one for every occasion, don't you?

00:43:59.750 --> 00:44:02.550
C'mon, let's eat. Our food's getting cold.

00:44:09.860 --> 00:44:12.090
Hey, look! He looks just like Alphonse!

00:44:13.130 --> 00:44:17.430
Alphonse the First. He was my dog when I was little.

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:19.900
What? So your '98 is the Second?

00:44:20.440 --> 00:44:24.300
Nice try, he's the Third. Alphonse the Second was a cat.

00:44:26.170 --> 00:44:27.330
What's he doing?

00:44:27.740 --> 00:44:33.310
He probably hears something. Dogs can hear things we can't.

00:44:33.750 --> 00:44:36.480
I bet the wind is howling somewhere.

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:39.650
Howling wind, huh...

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:43.420
What's wrong? Why are you making that face?

00:44:44.630 --> 00:44:45.650
<i>Winds...</i>

00:44:47.200 --> 00:44:48.420
<i>Sounds...</i>

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:52.160
<i>Sounds humans</i> <i>can't hear...</i>

00:44:55.070 --> 00:44:58.630
Noa, I need the scooter. I gotta check on something right away.

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:02.100
What? How am I supposed to get back, then?

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:06.510
Another thing. Tell the Commander to expect a call from me.

00:45:06.820 --> 00:45:07.940
Gotta go!

00:45:08.650 --> 00:45:09.740
Hey, wait!

00:45:09.850 --> 00:45:14.690
You jerk! Asuma, I HATE YOUR GUTS!

00:45:23.870 --> 00:45:25.660
I couldn't reach my old man.

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:28.700
And the company won't tell me anything, either.

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:32.870
I called home and they said he hasn't been there for a while.

00:45:32.980 --> 00:45:35.140
Your father has always been very busy.

00:45:35.240 --> 00:45:39.980
Sorry for the mess. My daughter's here with her son.

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:41.110
Your grandson, huh?

00:45:41.280 --> 00:45:45.050
I guess you can't have beer on duty. I'll make some coffee.

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:51.660
Actually, I want you to tell me what sound frequency is making those Labor go berserk.

00:45:58.030 --> 00:46:00.260
The Sumitomo Building in the Shinjuku New Metropolitan Center.

00:46:00.570 --> 00:46:03.160
The twin highrise at Riverside Parktown 21.

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:07.440
Both tall skyscrapers with large atriums that act like huge whistles.

00:46:07.740 --> 00:46:10.270
The wind blowing past them creates low-frequency sounds.

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:14.620
Plus the harp bridge at Ariake connecting Daiba and Shibaura.

00:46:14.720 --> 00:46:18.350
The supporting cables must hum like a giant wind harp.

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:20.180
Sounds that humans can't hear...

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:23.020
Sounds that only the sensors of Labors can pick up.

00:46:23.330 --> 00:46:26.390
That's the trigger for the Labor malfunctions.

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:33.630
Labors operate in cities and construction sites where sounds like these are common.

00:46:33.870 --> 00:46:35.600
I have to hand it to Hoba,

00:46:35.700 --> 00:46:38.640
triggering them with low-frequency sounds was brilliant.

00:46:39.210 --> 00:46:44.580
I'm sure it wasn't a coincidence that he joined a Labor latecomer like SHI.

00:46:44.680 --> 00:46:49.170
Dad was up against the big boys, and would grab any chance to corner the market.

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:51.750
Naturally, he went for the bait - HOS.

00:46:52.090 --> 00:46:55.110
Now he's panicking because he knows he's been conned.

00:46:56.020 --> 00:46:59.820
Just like he wanted, HOS has become the standard for all Labors,

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:03.130
but that's gonna be Shinohara Heavy Industries' undoing.

00:47:04.170 --> 00:47:07.030
I can't reach him 'cause he's scrambling around trying to fix it, right?

00:47:07.140 --> 00:47:10.330
If that's made public now, Asuma, SHI is as good as-

00:47:10.670 --> 00:47:11.660
Uncle Jitsuyama!

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:18.640
If not me, someone else will figure it out eventually.

00:47:18.910 --> 00:47:22.650
I want you to be the one who breaks the news.

00:47:23.990 --> 00:47:27.080
We can't let Labors become the bad guys, right?

00:47:27.260 --> 00:47:31.220
LaBor

00:47:48.580 --> 00:47:50.270
Asuma!

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:54.210
Hey! I'm back!

00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:56.710
Asuma!

00:47:56.850 --> 00:47:59.080
Rejoice, Noa! We cleared it up!

00:47:59.190 --> 00:48:02.210
Your noble sacrifice was not in vain!

00:48:02.620 --> 00:48:04.220
Asuma!

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:06.750
Asuma!

00:48:13.070 --> 00:48:16.040
Officer Shinohara, you are hereby suspended for two weeks!

00:48:16.770 --> 00:48:19.240
I assume I don't have to spell out why.

00:48:19.670 --> 00:48:20.640
I'm afraid you will, sir!

00:48:20.740 --> 00:48:21.830
All right.

00:48:21.940 --> 00:48:23.880
AWOL while on watch.

00:48:23.980 --> 00:48:27.240
Intrusion into a private residence and coercing a confession.

00:48:27.350 --> 00:48:29.250
Unauthorized use of the section's mainframe.

00:48:29.350 --> 00:48:32.220
Riding double with a colleague. Riding without a helmet.

00:48:32.320 --> 00:48:34.190
Drinking and driving. Numerous other traffic violations.

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:35.450
Shall I go on?

00:48:35.760 --> 00:48:40.850
Such flagrant violations warrant a disciplinary hearing, but given the circumstances...

00:48:40.960 --> 00:48:44.420
...and considering Cmdr. Gotoh's appeals, I've decided to handle this quietly.

00:48:44.530 --> 00:48:48.300
Be grateful he is selflessly willing to cover for his men!

00:48:48.670 --> 00:48:49.690
Dismissed!

00:48:49.800 --> 00:48:52.470
Sir, one question! What will happen with SHI?

00:48:52.570 --> 00:48:54.270
That is none of your concern!

00:48:54.940 --> 00:48:58.140
However, I suppose you would like some closure.

00:48:58.580 --> 00:49:02.140
Frankly, affairs like this are above your level.

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:10.150
Late last night, CEO Shinohara reported to MITI that HOS contained a major flaw.

00:49:10.490 --> 00:49:14.660
Due to the gravity of the matter, an emergency cabinet meeting was held this morning.

00:49:14.830 --> 00:49:21.740
The cabinet accepted SHI's proposal to reinstall the previous OS in all affected Labors.

00:49:21.940 --> 00:49:29.810
And to avoid any public commotion, this process will be billed as a free upgrade.

00:49:29.950 --> 00:49:33.210
That's the dumbest thing I've ever heard! What about their liability?!

00:49:33.320 --> 00:49:38.080
They get off scot-free while I get busted?! You're supposed to settle disputes fairly!

00:49:38.290 --> 00:49:40.750
You little punk! What do you think the police are for?!

00:49:40.860 --> 00:49:43.220
Investigations are NOT for resolving family squabbles!

00:49:43.330 --> 00:49:45.030
- Like you have the moral high ground!

00:49:44.130 --> 00:49:45.390
- Anyway, Security Bureau officers aren't...

00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:47.100
- You guys know the truth, but you're...

00:49:45.490 --> 00:49:46.530
- ...authorized to conduct investigations!

00:49:46.630 --> 00:49:49.160
- It's inexcusable that you went ahead...

00:49:47.200 --> 00:49:52.560
- ...dealing under the table to cover your asses!

00:49:49.260 --> 00:49:50.570
- ...without the permission of your superiors!

00:49:57.840 --> 00:50:00.540
I guess they figured they'd handle the damage control themselves...

00:50:00.740 --> 00:50:03.970
...instead of waiting to become the subject of an external exposé.

00:50:04.810 --> 00:50:11.010
The government is less worried about SHI's guilt than about public suspicion of Labors.

00:50:11.420 --> 00:50:13.610
After all, Labors are integral to the Babylon Project.

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:17.180
If that hit the fan, their asses would be on the line.

00:50:17.530 --> 00:50:21.480
So they overlooked SHI's guilt in order to bury the whole mess.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:23.590
That about sums it up.

00:50:23.800 --> 00:50:27.060
C'mon, cheer up. You did some fine work.

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:31.630
Sure, the data was there, but you exposed HOS' dangers by yourself.

00:50:32.010 --> 00:50:33.630
Your endgame was a little weak, that's all.

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:38.140
I wonder how the Chief even knew about us riding double...

00:50:38.250 --> 00:50:41.840
Oh, that? I made Izumi talk, and I ratted you out.

00:50:42.550 --> 00:50:43.850
Commander!

00:50:44.690 --> 00:50:46.090
You were exhausted, right?

00:50:46.190 --> 00:50:49.620
Now that you're not on duty, you can focus on your investigation.

00:50:49.720 --> 00:50:52.420
You still wanna go ahead with that, Commander?

00:50:52.660 --> 00:50:55.220
Jeez, a kid your age should be more tenacious.

00:50:55.660 --> 00:51:00.260
Not even the guys at SHI know what's inside HOS.

00:51:00.670 --> 00:51:06.540
If a simple OS reinstall would fix it, why would Hoba bother to kill himself?

00:51:06.640 --> 00:51:07.730
Am I right?

00:51:08.380 --> 00:51:12.470
Then you still buy the "Ei'ichi Hoba Conspiracy Theory", sir?

00:51:12.580 --> 00:51:14.570
What's your take on it, Shinohara?

00:51:15.120 --> 00:51:17.110
I have to agree.

00:51:17.650 --> 00:51:18.710
Asuma!

00:51:20.120 --> 00:51:22.650
Our man's back in town.

00:51:23.460 --> 00:51:24.520
Mr. Shige!

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:26.290
Hello!

00:51:28.030 --> 00:51:29.160
What's with the getup?

00:51:29.260 --> 00:51:31.530
I could ask you the same thing.

00:51:31.630 --> 00:51:35.870
Oh, things got a little nuts, and I got two weeks' suspension.

00:51:35.970 --> 00:51:38.770
That's what you get for not being a team player!

00:51:39.470 --> 00:51:42.600
If you had let us in on it, none of this would've happened.

00:51:42.710 --> 00:51:46.170
I never thought I'd hear you say the words "team player"!

00:51:46.380 --> 00:51:47.470
Oh, yeah?!

00:51:47.580 --> 00:51:52.280
Fellas, please. I just got back from overseas.

00:51:52.390 --> 00:51:54.050
Okay? For my sake, please, let it go.

00:51:54.420 --> 00:51:57.950
Mr. Shige, you have to file for an OS reinstall! Hurry!

00:51:58.060 --> 00:52:01.030
Yeah, about that! Sorry to put you right to work, but could you?

00:52:01.130 --> 00:52:02.760
- Do a reinstall?
- Yeah!

00:52:02.860 --> 00:52:05.060
- On the '98s?
- Their OS!

00:52:07.200 --> 00:52:10.690
What's the problem?! It's dangerous to leave HOS installed!

00:52:10.910 --> 00:52:14.360
I worked myself to death to get this far! I even got suspended!

00:52:14.840 --> 00:52:16.810
I never installed HOS on them.

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:19.180
What?! But headquarters ordered you to!

00:52:19.280 --> 00:52:21.310
I just pretended to install it.

00:52:21.420 --> 00:52:25.650
I faked the activation display and sent back the diskette.

00:52:26.090 --> 00:52:28.450
The OS is the same one you've always had.

00:52:28.560 --> 00:52:30.850
There weren't any problems, right?

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:35.360
Y'know, when I heard HOS was fishy, I was like, "Hell, yeah!"

00:52:35.460 --> 00:52:38.090
I never have done it if not for the wise teachings of my good ol' Cap!

00:52:38.200 --> 00:52:42.660
I don't care how good it sounds! I'm not gonna install some totally unknown system.

00:52:42.770 --> 00:52:45.260
I figured if anything went wrong, I'd take the heat.

00:52:45.370 --> 00:52:47.810
So off I went to the US, my lips firmly sealed!

00:52:48.180 --> 00:52:50.740
Boy, did I make the right call or what?!

00:52:51.050 --> 00:52:53.540
Huh? None of you guys knew?

00:52:54.420 --> 00:52:56.040
Now, that's strange...

00:52:56.450 --> 00:52:59.910
When Mr. Gotoh called me, I told him all about it.

00:53:00.020 --> 00:53:01.350
You told the Commander?

00:53:01.660 --> 00:53:04.420
I think I get it. He knew that if he kept quiet,

00:53:04.530 --> 00:53:07.150
Mr. Asuma would work nonstop to solve this case.

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:10.350
It sounds like one of our Commander's schemes.

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:12.730
He has no idea what he put Noa and me through...

00:53:12.830 --> 00:53:17.100
Messing with our youthful idealism... That rotten, middle-aged bastard...

00:53:17.670 --> 00:53:19.300
Poor Asuma...

00:53:19.670 --> 00:53:22.370
CURSE THAT LOUSY BASTARD!

00:53:35.420 --> 00:53:39.430
H o b a

00:54:30.980 --> 00:54:33.060
Ei'ichi Hoba 1st Grade, Class 3

00:54:35.850 --> 00:54:36.910
Mr. Matsui!

00:54:43.760 --> 00:54:44.590
Look!

00:54:44.830 --> 00:54:46.050
It's in English...

00:54:46.590 --> 00:54:49.320
"He bowed the heavens also,"...

00:54:49.830 --> 00:54:56.070
"...and came down; and darkness was under his feet."

00:54:56.400 --> 00:54:58.740
That's out of Psalms.

00:55:00.310 --> 00:55:03.640
It's a good bet that Hoba wrote that there.

00:55:03.980 --> 00:55:07.540
I'll admit, I'm amazed that you tracked down the house he was born in.

00:55:07.650 --> 00:55:11.810
Nah, I just backtracked through all his moves, and wound up here.

00:55:12.150 --> 00:55:14.780
But this is it. A real dead end.

00:55:16.990 --> 00:55:22.950
During the '80s real estate bubble, land sharks did a number on that part of town.

00:55:23.260 --> 00:55:26.360
After the revised National Land Use Planning Act and whatnot,

00:55:26.470 --> 00:55:30.900
the deeds to that area sat in limbo and it was never redeveloped.

00:55:32.670 --> 00:55:35.370
But you know, this really is a strange city...

00:55:35.740 --> 00:55:42.740
As I tracked down his past, it felt like time was passing me by.

00:55:42.980 --> 00:55:48.080
Places I used to know were just rotting away, or had became empty ruins.

00:55:48.390 --> 00:55:51.090
Look away for even an instant and it's all gone...

00:55:51.390 --> 00:55:54.880
And it happens before I can even consider what it all means.

00:55:55.730 --> 00:56:00.100
I guess the past is worthless in this city.

00:56:00.200 --> 00:56:05.370
This spot was part of the ocean until recently.

00:56:05.470 --> 00:56:09.910
And in a few years, a huge city will rise up from the sea in front of us.

00:56:10.240 --> 00:56:14.840
But before you know it, that will become yesterday's junk, too.

00:56:15.480 --> 00:56:18.280
It's like sitting through a sick joke.

00:56:19.050 --> 00:56:23.250
Maybe that's what Hoba wanted us to see.

00:56:26.760 --> 00:56:30.630
"Where are we going? Who are we?"

00:56:30.730 --> 00:56:31.890
What's that from?

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:35.730
Long ago, a barbarian force invaded Europe and destroyed everything in sight.

00:56:35.840 --> 00:56:38.570
Their commander wrote that on a wall.

00:56:41.540 --> 00:56:44.410
Not like that, you bozo! Snap it back! Snap it!

00:56:44.650 --> 00:56:45.440
Well, gotta go.

00:56:45.550 --> 00:56:47.780
Thanks. Sorry for making you go through all this.

00:56:55.990 --> 00:56:59.930
One last thing. What's your take on the guy?

00:57:00.230 --> 00:57:03.660
He's our man, Mr. Gotoh. No doubt about it.

00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:09.930
Problem is, my crew can't do anything more if he's already dead.

00:57:10.140 --> 00:57:13.230
Well, I'll pay you back one of these days. Psychologically.

00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:15.970
Would you get a load of these guys?

00:57:16.080 --> 00:57:20.170
A tomato patch, chicken coops, and the maintenance crew is all out fishing.

00:57:20.720 --> 00:57:24.150
Are these guys really police officers?

00:57:24.250 --> 00:57:29.050
Not exactly police. More like "superheroes".

00:57:31.180 --> 00:57:33.910
Say, do you still think I'm reading too much into this?

00:57:35.220 --> 00:57:36.620
Well, it's possible.

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:42.020
I'm thankful the problem was spotted before the Type Zeroes arrived.

00:57:42.130 --> 00:57:46.430
I had Kanuka in New York investigate Hoba's stay at MIT.

00:57:46.530 --> 00:57:49.160
Apparently, his nickname over there was "Jehovah".

00:57:49.470 --> 00:57:50.560
Jehovah?

00:57:50.670 --> 00:57:53.070
That's God's name in the Old Testament, if I'm not mistaken.

00:57:53.710 --> 00:57:56.940
His name does sound similar. I suppose it shows how brilliant he was.

00:57:57.140 --> 00:57:59.630
By the way, there's a bit more.

00:58:00.050 --> 00:58:04.110
The proper pronunciation is actually "Yah-veh" or "Yah-ha-weh".

00:58:04.920 --> 00:58:07.940
They say "Jehovah" was an incorrect pronunciation that took root.

00:58:09.350 --> 00:58:12.260
Word is, Hoba was delighted to learn that.

00:58:13.530 --> 00:58:19.860
You know, Ms. Shinobu, I think I'm getting a handle on how this guy's mind worked.

00:58:20.430 --> 00:58:26.270
Day after day, he looked over the skyscrapers, endlessly honing his plot.

00:58:27.910 --> 00:58:32.400
"And the Lord came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of men builded."

00:58:34.750 --> 00:58:38.880
"Go to, let us go down, and there confound their language,"

00:58:39.080 --> 00:58:42.350
"that they may not understand one another's speech."

00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:48.020
"Therefore is the name of it called Babel"...

00:58:51.160 --> 00:58:52.790
Couldn't that be a coincidence?

00:58:52.900 --> 00:58:56.390
You think Hoba plunging to his death from the Ark is also a coincidence?

00:58:56.600 --> 00:59:03.240
If his whole plan centers around the Bible, he'd never pass up a target like the Babylon Project.

00:59:04.040 --> 00:59:06.030
"Babylon is fallen, is fallen;"

00:59:06.350 --> 00:59:09.370
"and all the graven images of her gods he hath broken unto the ground."

00:59:09.650 --> 00:59:11.380
The list goes on and on.

00:59:11.680 --> 00:59:13.980
Hoba went out of his way to leave these messages behind.

00:59:14.220 --> 00:59:15.880
And then there's this-

00:59:16.860 --> 00:59:22.730
Hoba's 26 residences were all located in the three spots that Asuma identified.

00:59:23.160 --> 00:59:28.160
It had Mr. Matsui perplexed, since Hoba obviously wanted someone to solve his riddle.

00:59:28.270 --> 00:59:31.240
- Is it a challenge?
- He's not that sort of romantic.

00:59:31.840 --> 00:59:34.100
He never considered the police to be worthy opponents.

00:59:34.910 --> 00:59:38.270
He had absolute confidence in his program.

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:42.240
Otherwise, he wouldn't have killed himself before seeing the outcome.

00:59:42.920 --> 00:59:48.290
My guess is, on the day he took his dive, he was gleefully sneering at us.

00:59:48.490 --> 00:59:50.850
No, at everyone in this city.

00:59:52.190 --> 00:59:58.150
Say, did you know you look almost giddy whenever you talk about Hoba?

00:59:58.260 --> 01:00:00.730
Really? Uh-oh, that can't be good...

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:02.260
Seriously?

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:08.630
If this is such an epic crime, do you think those two are up to cracking it?

01:00:08.740 --> 01:00:10.330
Sure, why not?

01:00:19.820 --> 01:00:22.810
I'm sleepy... 'N hungry... 'N tired...

01:00:22.920 --> 01:00:25.360
Okay, data entry's done.

01:00:29.260 --> 01:00:30.890
And away we go... Fire.

01:00:35.900 --> 01:00:38.200
What are the simulation parameters?

01:00:38.400 --> 01:00:42.570
Well, if 10.21 Hz is the right frequency, this should be it.

01:00:42.670 --> 01:00:45.670
So the vulnerable areas would only extend this far...

01:00:46.080 --> 01:00:49.210
Well, then I can see why they'd deal under the table to cover it up.

01:00:50.320 --> 01:00:53.810
Man, this doesn't make any sense...

01:00:58.690 --> 01:01:01.250
There's no way it's limited to just this...

01:01:01.560 --> 01:01:03.930
What are we missing in the data...?

01:01:04.030 --> 01:01:07.930
If we could crack those files that're inside HOS,

01:01:08.030 --> 01:01:12.660
we'd be able to prove Mr. Gotoh's "Criminal Intent by Hoba" theory.

01:01:12.970 --> 01:01:16.240
Then we'd be able to muster more manpower and better facilities.

01:01:16.340 --> 01:01:18.000
You think they're a Trojan horse?

01:01:18.110 --> 01:01:19.370
Most likely...

01:01:19.580 --> 01:01:22.840
That OS reinstall probably spread the virus even further.

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:25.240
Man, that was such a stupid decision...

01:01:25.720 --> 01:01:27.740
Mr. Shige! You have a visitor!

01:01:27.850 --> 01:01:29.080
Thanks!

01:01:30.160 --> 01:01:31.420
I brought you a care package!

01:01:31.520 --> 01:01:33.460
So, have you figured out anything yet?

01:01:33.560 --> 01:01:35.860
Just how powerless we are...

01:01:36.060 --> 01:01:37.650
That's all, huh?

01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:40.260
- Here's your paper.
- Thanks.

01:01:40.370 --> 01:01:43.060
Okay, let's see what's happening in the world...

01:01:43.170 --> 01:01:48.070
Against a computer god like him, I'm just a teenage computer geek.

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:50.400
Noa, get me some coffee, okay?

01:01:50.510 --> 01:01:53.030
That would hit the spot... Sorry, I only have instant.

01:01:53.150 --> 01:01:54.480
Sure, coming right up.

01:02:05.590 --> 01:02:07.580
- Noa, turn the burner back on!
- What?

01:02:07.690 --> 01:02:08.680
Just do it!

01:02:08.790 --> 01:02:10.560
Jeez, you don't have to order me around...

01:02:14.230 --> 01:02:16.130
What? What about it?!

01:02:26.040 --> 01:02:28.040
- What's gotten into you?
- Resonance, man! Resonance!

01:02:29.280 --> 01:02:32.770
It was pointless to run a simulation based on separate sources!

01:02:33.090 --> 01:02:36.150
When you get a resonance between objects with the same resonant frequency,

01:02:36.360 --> 01:02:39.260
the vibrations interfere and get amplified!

01:02:44.000 --> 01:02:45.490
Doesn't look all that different...

01:02:45.760 --> 01:02:49.760
Problem is, the low-frequency waves that set it off are too weak.

01:02:49.900 --> 01:02:52.600
We gotta kick-start it with something much stronger.

01:02:52.700 --> 01:02:54.330
Buildings with large atriums, harp bridges...

01:02:54.440 --> 01:02:57.430
Exposed I-beams at construction sites, air ducts for underground facilities...

01:02:57.540 --> 01:02:59.130
There has to be something else!

01:02:59.410 --> 01:03:02.610
Ideally, you'd want a wide, open space with no obstructions.

01:03:02.710 --> 01:03:04.880
A building with lots of cavities...

01:03:04.980 --> 01:03:07.080
Some kind of multilevel structure...

01:03:07.220 --> 01:03:09.740
Where would you find anything like that in crowded city like-

01:03:23.600 --> 01:03:25.370
The Ark?!

01:03:25.740 --> 01:03:27.400
How could we have missed that?!

01:03:27.740 --> 01:03:30.230
I forgot that I copied its schematics at Hachioji.

01:03:30.640 --> 01:03:33.480
Let's slap this structure into the wind tunnel simulation,

01:03:33.850 --> 01:03:36.970
but set the wind speed to "undefined", and see what happens.

01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:39.880
This needs more horsepower than I have here!

01:03:42.890 --> 01:03:44.650
Okay, we're networked!

01:03:53.300 --> 01:03:56.170
- It sure is taking its time...
- Here it comes!

01:04:02.440 --> 01:04:04.710
Holy crap...

01:04:06.340 --> 01:04:08.010
What's the wind velocity for this?

01:04:09.280 --> 01:04:10.940
40 meters per second?!

01:04:11.120 --> 01:04:15.210
Aw, man... You only get winds like that in a typhoon-

01:04:22.560 --> 01:04:27.430
...and so, the powerful typhoon #19 is currently off the southwest tip of the Kii Peninsula,

01:04:27.530 --> 01:04:30.870
heading northeast at 25 km/h.

01:04:31.070 --> 01:04:34.100
It should hit Tokyo Bay after midnight the day after tomorrow.

01:04:34.410 --> 01:04:36.310
The typhoon's air pressure is 950 millibars,

01:04:36.410 --> 01:04:40.850
and areas within 20 km of its center can expect wind speeds over 40 m/sec.

01:04:41.110 --> 01:04:42.410
Enough, already! Stop this!

01:04:42.510 --> 01:04:45.640
This is preposterous! This is all speculation!

01:04:46.690 --> 01:04:50.050
As I understand it, this simulation was compiled by a suspended officer...

01:04:50.160 --> 01:04:53.120
...with the help of one of your maintenance crew.

01:04:53.230 --> 01:04:56.850
Do you really expect us to take this seriously?

01:04:57.800 --> 01:05:00.770
According to the Meteorological Agency's forecast,

01:05:00.870 --> 01:05:03.560
typhoon #19 will hit the Tokyo area after midnight two days from now.

01:05:03.670 --> 01:05:06.140
We don't have enough time to run another simulation for verification.

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:13.080
You may say this is mere conjecture, but there is nothing to contradict it, either.

01:05:14.780 --> 01:05:18.220
The typhoon winds will cause the Ark to howl out loud.

01:05:18.520 --> 01:05:24.180
That will then cause hundreds of waterfront skyscrapers to emit a low-frequency bellow,

01:05:24.490 --> 01:05:26.980
thus causing the 8000 Labors in the Tokyo area to go berserk.

01:05:27.460 --> 01:05:31.290
I shouldn't have to tell you how much damage will result.

01:05:31.630 --> 01:05:34.960
Labors are used not only along the waterfront and the inner-city redevelopment zones,

01:05:35.070 --> 01:05:37.330
but also in construction projects 1000 meters underground,

01:05:37.440 --> 01:05:41.870
and even within the cores of certain nuclear reactors.

01:05:43.380 --> 01:05:53.620
If this scenario comes to pass, who will take responsibility for not preventing this disaster?

01:05:53.920 --> 01:05:55.390
Or rather, this criminal plot?

01:05:55.490 --> 01:06:00.860
Gotoh, the Labors in the Tokyo area have been downgraded to their original OS's.

01:06:01.160 --> 01:06:05.120
Even assuming that your Hoba conspiracy theory is true,

01:06:05.460 --> 01:06:08.300
wouldn't the reinstallations have foiled his plans?

01:06:08.730 --> 01:06:13.730
We are working with MIT to crack HOS, but its true nature remains a mystery.

01:06:14.210 --> 01:06:17.040
Their experts also uniformly agree that the likelihood...

01:06:17.140 --> 01:06:20.130
...of a dormant virus in the Labors' subsystems cannot be ignored.

01:06:20.380 --> 01:06:26.180
In which case, we should assume any Labor that has had contact with HOS is infected.

01:06:26.790 --> 01:06:30.450
Should we alter the path of the typhoon? Chop down hundreds of skyscrapers?

01:06:30.820 --> 01:06:34.120
Should we dismantle over 8000 Labors? Or...

01:06:35.260 --> 01:06:38.530
We have four options. Your decision, please.

01:06:40.870 --> 01:06:44.430
From midnight tomorrow until the typhoon has completely passed,

01:06:44.540 --> 01:06:47.700
all Labors within city limits are to be deactivated.

01:06:47.870 --> 01:06:51.500
Labor production lines and repair facilities must also be shut down.

01:06:51.610 --> 01:06:54.840
Explain the situation to the Fire Defense Agency, the Defense Agency,

01:06:54.950 --> 01:06:57.880
as well as neighboring prefectures, and request assistance.

01:06:57.980 --> 01:06:58.950
Dismissed.

01:07:00.490 --> 01:07:04.010
Sir, there is a real possibility that the low-frequency vibrations...

01:07:04.190 --> 01:07:06.680
...might cause deactivated Labors to start up again.

01:07:06.960 --> 01:07:10.160
Have you forgotten the incident with the Self-Defense Forces experimental Labor?

01:07:10.260 --> 01:07:11.020
Gotoh!

01:07:11.130 --> 01:07:14.190
Incident? I wasn't informed of any incident.

01:07:14.900 --> 01:07:16.530
In that case, I have one more question.

01:07:16.670 --> 01:07:18.660
You've said enough already!

01:07:19.470 --> 01:07:22.670
If anything happened as a result of a typhoon, no matter what that might be,

01:07:22.770 --> 01:07:25.940
no one could be held responsible, could they?

01:07:26.340 --> 01:07:28.740
After all, it's something that the typhoon did.

01:07:31.420 --> 01:07:34.250
Agreed. An act of God cannot be helped.

01:07:34.450 --> 01:07:35.440
But, sir!

01:07:37.520 --> 01:07:40.010
Assistant Inspector Gotoh returning to post, sir!

01:07:41.590 --> 01:07:42.820
What was all that about?

01:07:42.930 --> 01:07:46.060
Like he said, it can't be helped if it's an act of God.

01:07:46.160 --> 01:07:49.570
If we can prove Hoba's crime afterwards, fine.

01:07:49.870 --> 01:07:53.770
If we can't, then we'll just play dumb and blame the typhoon.

01:07:53.870 --> 01:07:57.240
If it gets out that we did it, the Chief will get the blame.

01:07:57.680 --> 01:07:59.270
That sly old fox...

01:07:59.380 --> 01:08:00.640
I feel sorry for the Chief...

01:08:00.750 --> 01:08:05.410
Save your sympathy for US. If that happens, they'll say we're criminals.

01:08:06.020 --> 01:08:07.810
Think we should call it quits?

01:08:07.920 --> 01:08:11.860
No, sir. I'd rather regret something done than undone.

01:08:11.960 --> 01:08:12.890
Then it's settled!

01:08:17.900 --> 01:08:21.760
I won't be back for a while, so follow Ms. Nagumo's orders until then.

01:08:21.870 --> 01:08:22.730
Yes, sir!

01:08:25.200 --> 01:08:27.170
What's about to happen?

01:08:27.740 --> 01:08:30.170
We're gonna tear down the Ark!

01:08:35.310 --> 01:08:38.870
Start the waterproofing after the memory banks and cockpit area are insulated!

01:08:38.980 --> 01:08:39.880
The clock's ticking, slackers!

01:08:39.980 --> 01:08:42.010
Cap! The pontoons have arrived!

01:08:42.120 --> 01:08:43.880
Right, get Squad 3 to start working on 'em!

01:08:44.120 --> 01:08:46.320
I'll send you more people as they finish here! Bring 'em in!

01:08:46.460 --> 01:08:49.150
Break out the shotgun! I just got clearance to use it!

01:08:49.890 --> 01:08:52.090
Hey, you had some experimental explosive shells, right?

01:08:52.200 --> 01:08:53.890
Load 'em into cartridges, too.

01:08:54.330 --> 01:08:56.390
But those are still too dangerous to-

01:08:56.570 --> 01:08:57.830
JUST DO IT!

01:08:57.970 --> 01:09:04.340
Typhoon #19 is continuing northeast, and will hit the Kanto area early tomorrow morning.

01:09:05.280 --> 01:09:08.940
Here it comes, here it comes...! Here comes the storm!

01:09:09.150 --> 01:09:14.380
It looks like we'll have to jettison sections using the fire-emergency system.

01:09:15.220 --> 01:09:18.660
Which means we need to secure the central control room...

01:09:19.020 --> 01:09:20.420
Show me the file on the security robots.

01:09:40.780 --> 01:09:45.510
The Labors are set up, and the pontoons should be attached to the carriers in a few hours.

01:09:45.780 --> 01:09:48.410
What I'd like to know is how you got hold of something so drastic...

01:09:48.360 --> 01:09:52.380
Stop the Babylon Project Now!

01:09:49.220 --> 01:09:51.920
I pestered the airborne Labor unit at Kasumigaura.

01:09:52.490 --> 01:09:56.320
When I mentioned that experimental Labor, they lent it to us, no questions asked.

01:09:56.630 --> 01:09:58.290
I'm on my way out to Narita.

01:09:58.400 --> 01:10:02.990
They might have to fight every Labor inside the Ark, so I called in a secret weapon.

01:10:03.230 --> 01:10:05.570
As a matter of fact, yes, I did get her to come.

01:10:16.150 --> 01:10:17.110
Sightseeing?

01:10:17.220 --> 01:10:18.200
No.

01:10:21.290 --> 01:10:22.380
Combat.

01:10:39.770 --> 01:10:41.570
Inflate the pontoons!

01:10:50.150 --> 01:10:56.680
There is nothing left to say. The Ark has been completely evacuated.

01:10:56.990 --> 01:10:58.480
Get out there and give 'em hell!

01:11:30.360 --> 01:11:33.650
Are you sure about this? It's not too late to call them back.

01:11:34.090 --> 01:11:37.580
Even if we succeed, if we can't prove Hoba's criminal intent-

01:11:38.030 --> 01:11:40.260
Then we'd be the criminals.

01:11:40.600 --> 01:11:46.760
But if we sit on our hands and all hell breaks loose, we'll still be criminals.

01:11:46.870 --> 01:11:48.170
Which would you prefer?

01:11:49.640 --> 01:11:53.630
Besides, we never had a choice to begin with.

01:11:54.410 --> 01:12:00.510
If we fail, the entire bay area will be in ruins. I can't imagine the extent of the damage.

01:12:01.250 --> 01:12:06.880
Even if we do succeed, the Babylon Project will be crippled by the loss of the Ark.

01:12:07.530 --> 01:12:09.620
It's a no-win situation.

01:12:10.800 --> 01:12:15.390
I suppose the outcome was largely decided when he jumped into the sea.

01:12:15.870 --> 01:12:17.430
Don't you think?

01:12:17.540 --> 01:12:19.060
And you still want to do this?

01:12:19.170 --> 01:12:21.100
Not much of a cop, am I?

01:12:21.710 --> 01:12:24.610
I feel bad about the raw deals you get because of me.

01:12:24.780 --> 01:12:26.180
Where are you going?

01:12:26.540 --> 01:12:28.710
I might have gotten tacit approval from headquarters,

01:12:28.810 --> 01:12:31.680
but we're gonna put on one hell of a fireworks show in the middle of Tokyo Bay.

01:12:32.080 --> 01:12:34.850
I'm sure the Maritime Safety Agency and the Ports and Harbors Bureau...

01:12:34.950 --> 01:12:36.440
...will have something to say about it.

01:12:36.690 --> 01:12:39.320
I'm gonna head over there and buy my people some time.

01:12:39.660 --> 01:12:41.680
It's part of my job as their commander and all...

01:12:42.330 --> 01:12:43.160
Mr. Gotoh...

01:12:43.260 --> 01:12:44.190
Yes?

01:12:44.300 --> 01:12:45.560
Never mind. It's nothing.

01:12:45.660 --> 01:12:48.190
Well, make the rescue arrangements, okay?

01:13:07.250 --> 01:13:09.080
There it is! 500 meters dead ahead!

01:13:09.190 --> 01:13:10.520
It's the Ark!

01:13:51.160 --> 01:13:53.760
Doesn't this place make you a little uneasy?

01:13:53.870 --> 01:13:56.360
It's like a pagan shrine full of idols, isn't it?

01:13:56.600 --> 01:13:59.760
A fitting place for a man who's passing himself off as Jehovah.

01:13:59.940 --> 01:14:01.030
Listen up, people.

01:14:01.140 --> 01:14:03.940
The guard robots aren't much trouble if you keep clear,

01:14:04.040 --> 01:14:07.070
but there's a lot of them, so try to avoid combat as much as possible!

01:14:07.180 --> 01:14:08.870
Our main objective is securing the control room.

01:14:09.450 --> 01:14:12.040
Oota, you take point! Noa, cover our rear!

01:14:12.950 --> 01:14:15.980
We'll run flat-out all the way! Charge!

01:14:24.660 --> 01:14:26.390
C'mon, you worthless hunks of junk!

01:14:26.500 --> 01:14:28.590
You wanna piece of me?! I'm right here!

01:14:54.690 --> 01:14:56.090
Five to the rear!

01:14:56.390 --> 01:14:58.660
Now there's eight! They keep increasing!

01:15:03.570 --> 01:15:04.560
Wait for me!

01:15:04.700 --> 01:15:05.630
Move it!

01:15:07.140 --> 01:15:08.540
Eat hot lead!

01:15:14.110 --> 01:15:15.700
I said to avoid combat!

01:15:15.810 --> 01:15:18.840
We did. As much as possible.

01:15:19.280 --> 01:15:21.310
In the field, you play it by ear!

01:15:21.420 --> 01:15:23.610
The situation on the ground is always fluid!

01:15:24.790 --> 01:15:28.380
Look, just watch your ammo! We don't know what to expect!

01:15:28.490 --> 01:15:29.460
Roger!

01:15:50.180 --> 01:15:51.310
There it is!

01:16:02.560 --> 01:16:03.460
Move it!

01:16:03.560 --> 01:16:06.220
Jeez, does he have to smash everything?

01:16:06.360 --> 01:16:09.460
We gotta hurry, or we'll be overrun by every security robot in the Ark!

01:16:09.770 --> 01:16:11.430
Please start up the emergency systems!

01:16:11.540 --> 01:16:13.940
That should shut the security system down!

01:16:17.410 --> 01:16:19.070
Okay, let's get to it!

01:16:20.440 --> 01:16:23.040
This will sound an alarm on land.

01:16:23.150 --> 01:16:27.350
Well then, let's see if we'll be greeted as heroes or arrested as criminals...

01:16:27.450 --> 01:16:32.350
...when the Maritime Safety people get here after the storm.

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:38.190
Do you read me? I just deactivated the security systems!

01:16:38.660 --> 01:16:39.460
Roger!

01:16:39.900 --> 01:16:41.560
We'll stay here and stand guard!

01:16:43.900 --> 01:16:46.370
Okay, let's start tearin' this place apart!

01:16:46.870 --> 01:16:47.960
Hold on a second!

01:16:48.140 --> 01:16:51.870
Somebody's... Somebody's in the auxiliary control room at the very top of the platform!

01:16:51.980 --> 01:16:52.910
What?!

01:16:55.510 --> 01:16:58.380
There, it's right there! See? There's movement!

01:17:05.560 --> 01:17:06.580
Jehovah...

01:17:08.990 --> 01:17:11.430
- It can't be...
- He's dead... Right?

01:17:11.530 --> 01:17:13.020
Hoba's ghost?!

01:17:13.130 --> 01:17:16.620
Or some kind of trap he left behind... Can you call the room?

01:17:16.730 --> 01:17:22.260
No, the room isn't in use. You'd have to set up a line from there.

01:17:22.370 --> 01:17:24.200
So someone has to go up there, huh?

01:17:24.310 --> 01:17:28.270
There's no time! The typhoon's starting to make landfall!

01:17:35.350 --> 01:17:37.120
Noa! Do you read me?

01:17:37.220 --> 01:17:38.120
What's up?

01:17:38.220 --> 01:17:42.520
We have a situation. Someone might be in the auxiliary control room on the top floor.

01:17:42.730 --> 01:17:45.860
According to the system... it says it's Ei'ichi Hoba.

01:17:46.630 --> 01:17:48.330
This has to be a trap!

01:17:48.730 --> 01:17:53.000
Even so, there's someone up there! Noa, you handle this one, okay?

01:17:53.140 --> 01:17:54.300
You want me?!

01:17:54.410 --> 01:17:57.860
It's too dangerous without a Labor, and it might take some delicate decision-making.

01:17:57.980 --> 01:18:00.570
- Oota won't cut it!
- Say what?!

01:18:00.680 --> 01:18:03.200
Please, head up there! I'll back you up from here!

01:18:06.720 --> 01:18:10.710
"Forward and Backup are two-in-one", huh?

01:18:10.860 --> 01:18:11.510
One more thing!

01:18:11.620 --> 01:18:14.590
We're in a rush, so we'll proceed with our original plan at the same time!

01:18:14.690 --> 01:18:16.180
- What?!
- Do it!

01:18:23.900 --> 01:18:25.870
Is it true that they were finally able to crack HOS?

01:18:26.000 --> 01:18:27.830
Hey, Commander Nagumo.

01:18:28.570 --> 01:18:34.530
We just got word from MIT. They finally cracked those hidden files inside HOS.

01:18:34.650 --> 01:18:36.880
- So, what's the verdict?
- Criminal intent through and through.

01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:39.910
HOS carried an bona fide viral payload.

01:18:40.180 --> 01:18:46.920
It even infects the backup memory, and will rename itself and infect anything it can access.

01:18:47.290 --> 01:18:50.020
We should assume that any computer that's come into contact with HOS,

01:18:50.130 --> 01:18:52.860
or with a HOS-infected computer, should be considered infected.

01:18:53.000 --> 01:18:55.120
All hell's gonna break loose before the night's over.

01:18:55.230 --> 01:19:00.500
The people at MIT have started on a vaccine, but I doubt it'll be ready in time.

01:19:00.640 --> 01:19:04.600
If this had come in just 12 hours ago, we wouldn't have had to send our guys in alone.

01:19:04.880 --> 01:19:07.110
It's too late to send reinforcements.

01:19:07.210 --> 01:19:11.080
But we now have proof that Mr. Gotoh's theory was correct.

01:19:11.180 --> 01:19:12.810
At least we can get a rescue party to-

01:19:12.920 --> 01:19:14.110
Wait a minute!

01:19:14.650 --> 01:19:18.210
You said any computer that had contact with HOS, or with a HOS-infected computer?

01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:23.190
What about the main computer that they're using to dismantle the Ark?!

01:19:26.560 --> 01:19:32.300
Level D, Block 5, t-minus 30 seconds to jettison.

01:19:32.400 --> 01:19:40.280
All personnel in this block must evacuate immediately.

01:19:40.380 --> 01:19:47.580
All personnel in this block must evacuate immediately.

01:19:56.260 --> 01:20:00.250
D-5 Block, jettison complete. Alphonse moving from D-6 to D-7.

01:20:00.630 --> 01:20:03.190
Okay, let's get the next one ready. What's the code for D-7?

01:20:03.400 --> 01:20:05.390
FPD-00207.

01:20:05.740 --> 01:20:10.200
Why can't we just dump entire floors once she's cleared them?

01:20:10.370 --> 01:20:14.670
The Ark might become unbalanced if it loses too many blocks at once.

01:20:14.910 --> 01:20:20.540
Don't worry, dumping the exposed blocks first should limit the resonance effect quite a bit.

01:20:20.650 --> 01:20:22.050
How's the weather look?

01:20:23.050 --> 01:20:26.150
Wind speed, 35 m/sec... It just went up to 36!

01:20:26.520 --> 01:20:27.860
Okay, let's take care of D-7.

01:20:27.960 --> 01:20:33.230
Jettisoning of Level D, Block 7 is underway.

01:21:32.520 --> 01:21:34.550
Next! What's the code for E-2?

01:21:36.530 --> 01:21:38.360
Mr. Asuma! Look at that!

01:21:38.860 --> 01:21:41.490
Level D, Blocks 3, 5 and 7! Level C, too!

01:21:41.600 --> 01:21:44.090
Corridors on every level are full of Labors!

01:21:44.970 --> 01:21:48.340
But how?! The wind speed hasn't hit 40 m/sec yet!

01:21:48.440 --> 01:21:50.200
Maybe our simulation wasn't precise enough!

01:21:50.670 --> 01:21:54.240
Or maybe jettisoning certain sections caused additional wind eddies somehow!

01:21:55.950 --> 01:21:59.810
Come get some, you mindless rabble!

01:22:00.420 --> 01:22:01.540
Hiromi! What's going on?!

01:22:01.790 --> 01:22:04.380
There's a mob of berserk Labors across the bridge.

01:22:04.690 --> 01:22:06.680
Mr. Oota just rushed out to engage them.

01:22:07.020 --> 01:22:07.790
What about Kanuka?!

01:22:07.930 --> 01:22:10.260
She said she was going to go look for a weapon, but she hasn't come back.

01:22:10.360 --> 01:22:11.760
W-What should we do?!

01:22:11.860 --> 01:22:14.390
She wouldn't... No way!

01:22:15.400 --> 01:22:19.060
Boot okay. System normal.

01:22:19.740 --> 01:22:22.330
Type Zero, standing by.

01:22:25.110 --> 01:22:27.270
Kanuka, stop! The Type Zero's too dangerous!

01:22:27.380 --> 01:22:32.210
I just installed a quarantined system. With any luck, I'll have full control.

01:22:32.520 --> 01:22:34.210
But the virus might still be hiding in its system!

01:22:34.320 --> 01:22:36.680
Then it'll be a threat either way.

01:22:36.790 --> 01:22:39.920
Besides, we can't hold them off with just Oota's Labor 2.

01:22:40.020 --> 01:22:42.990
I'll take that chance. Clancy out.

01:22:59.910 --> 01:23:02.640
Noa, do you read me?! The situation's changed!

01:23:02.810 --> 01:23:04.870
The Labors have gone berserk sooner than expected!

01:23:05.150 --> 01:23:08.120
Time's running out! Get to the objective as fast as you can!

01:23:08.220 --> 01:23:09.150
Over!

01:23:09.250 --> 01:23:10.780
Damn it all!

01:24:39.040 --> 01:24:40.440
Birds...?

01:25:07.070 --> 01:25:09.670
The wind speed is now over 40 meters per second!

01:25:09.770 --> 01:25:10.930
We can't wait any longer!

01:25:11.040 --> 01:25:12.870
Noa! Come in, Noa!

01:25:13.440 --> 01:25:16.110
There's a raven... The ID badge is attached to its foot...

01:25:16.210 --> 01:25:19.550
Noa, just report your findings! Is anybody up there?!

01:25:19.650 --> 01:25:23.420
No, there aren't any PEOPLE up here!

01:25:29.730 --> 01:25:31.220
The system's not responding?

01:25:32.460 --> 01:25:35.020
Damn, even the main computer was infected!

01:25:35.870 --> 01:25:39.770
Control room! Yamazaki here! Control room, please respond!

01:25:40.500 --> 01:25:44.700
The mob of berserk Labors keeps on growing! I'm talking massive numbers!

01:25:44.940 --> 01:25:47.570
If they rush us, we won't stand a chance.

01:25:47.680 --> 01:25:49.980
Please, jettison the connecting areas right away!

01:25:53.580 --> 01:25:56.990
<i>There must be something</i> <i>we can do!</i>

01:25:59.520 --> 01:26:01.250
<i>A manual override...</i>

01:26:02.560 --> 01:26:06.760
Wasn't there a central access point to blow the explosive bolts for all the sectors?!

01:26:09.400 --> 01:26:12.670
But how are we going to get to it through that mess?

01:26:12.770 --> 01:26:14.000
Just find out where it is!

01:26:19.880 --> 01:26:21.740
It's at the top of the main shaft!

01:26:22.450 --> 01:26:24.240
It's right under the auxiliary control room?!

01:26:24.350 --> 01:26:29.340
It pays to be cops who value human life... We hit the jackpot, just in time.

01:26:29.990 --> 01:26:31.580
It's right under Noa's feet!

01:26:33.020 --> 01:26:36.360
You bastards- How ya like that?!

01:27:04.890 --> 01:27:05.950
The Type Zero?!

01:27:18.970 --> 01:27:20.400
Holy crap...!

01:27:30.410 --> 01:27:31.900
Mr. Oota! Ms. Kanuka!

01:27:32.280 --> 01:27:36.280
We're about to jettison all the floors at once! Return to the control room right now!

01:27:36.950 --> 01:27:38.650
Come in, Mr. Oota!

01:27:49.630 --> 01:27:52.900
30 seconds after ignition, all the floors should jettison in rapid succession!

01:27:53.600 --> 01:27:56.330
I have no idea what'll happen to the Ark when it loses that much mass so quickly.

01:27:56.610 --> 01:28:00.440
Listen Noa, DO NOT move from that spot after ignition! Got it?!

01:28:02.950 --> 01:28:04.440
Confirm the order, Noa!

01:28:04.680 --> 01:28:05.710
Roger that!

01:28:08.950 --> 01:28:10.080
Ignition!

01:28:13.590 --> 01:28:14.850
Ignition confirmed!

01:28:34.010 --> 01:28:35.100
Ms. Kanuka is-

01:30:54.950 --> 01:30:56.320
Asuma!

01:30:57.320 --> 01:30:58.840
Mr. Shinshi!

01:30:59.390 --> 01:31:00.580
Kanuka!

01:31:02.430 --> 01:31:03.860
Hiromi-chan!

01:31:04.530 --> 01:31:06.050
Mr. Oota!

01:31:09.470 --> 01:31:11.990
Somebody answer me!

01:31:18.880 --> 01:31:19.970
Mr. Oota...?

01:31:34.990 --> 01:31:37.860
Ow... Jeez, I never wanna go through-

01:31:46.070 --> 01:31:49.160
Noa, don't do it! Kanuka's in that thing!

01:32:06.790 --> 01:32:09.380
Kanuka! Kanuka!

01:32:10.130 --> 01:32:12.890
Kanuka! Answer me, Kanuka!

01:32:14.930 --> 01:32:16.630
Noa, ditch your Labor and run.

01:32:16.830 --> 01:32:18.930
You're no match for the Type Zero hand-to-hand.

01:32:19.200 --> 01:32:22.140
Can't you shut it down? What about the boot disk?

01:32:22.240 --> 01:32:24.970
I've already pulled it out and reset the system, and it still won't stop.

01:32:25.180 --> 01:32:30.440
The virus probably got into the pattern-study S-RAM and is rebuilding the OS from there.

01:32:30.580 --> 01:32:31.740
Where's it located?

01:32:31.850 --> 01:32:34.210
Same as your '98, behind the neck.

01:32:39.990 --> 01:32:41.150
Gotcha!

01:32:56.640 --> 01:32:59.110
Noa! Run! The '98 hasn't got a prayer!

01:33:17.060 --> 01:33:18.390
NOA!

01:34:08.510 --> 01:34:09.940
Damn you!

01:34:11.410 --> 01:34:13.710
SHUT DOWN!

01:34:33.970 --> 01:34:35.370
Noa!

01:34:39.540 --> 01:34:41.310
I did it...

01:34:42.280 --> 01:34:43.770
I did it...
